  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 110 AM MDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nThe upper high builds farther east and elongates west-to-east\nthrough Tuesday. Moisture profiles are not modeled to alter much,\nbut winds aloft will reduce slightly. This will keep a theta-E \nridge stretching from the southwestern mountains northeastward to \nthe northern peaks of NM, invoking a fairly climatological \ndistribution of slower moving afternoon storms. \n\nThe high elongates more across the southern states on Wednesday,\nnow shown to build farther west than previous model runs. This\ncould disrupt moisture over NM, introducing a slight drying while\npressure heights rise. Western and northern mountain ranges are\nstill modeled to initiate slow-moving cells with a stalled, weak \nbackdoor front being a fly in the ointment for far northeastern NM\nwhere convergence and upslope might provide a local enhancement \nto convection. \n\nInterestingly into Thursday the high skips westward into the\ndesert southwest states with heights reducing notably. The GFS and\nGEFS ensemble mean indicate some drying into NM from the\nnorthwest, but other deterministic and ensembles keep storms\nfiring over the western and northern mountains while expanding\ndown the central mountain chain. This trend continues into Friday\nand Saturday, as the GFS/GEFS allow drier westerlies to sag \nsouthward into NM. The largest ensemble cluster favors higher \nheights over NM, suggesting the GFS/GEFS is more of an outlier, \nbut overall precipitation signals are still quite noisy with only \nthe high terrain areas standing out as being more prone to storms.",
